# 2. THE CENTRAL IDEA: SEE THE UNIVERSE AT MULTIPLE SCALES

God's Eye View must provide an immersive, navigable representation of the cosmos.

The user should be able to transition between different astronomical scales.

Create the following observation layers.

## LAYER 1 — COSMIC OVERVIEW

A panoramic visualization of the large-scale universe.

Where suitable datasets and scientific evidence are available, represent:

- Large-scale galaxy distributions.
- Galaxy clusters and superclusters.
- Filaments and voids in the cosmic web.
- Observable-universe context.
- Important cosmic structures.
- The cosmic microwave background as a separate scientific visualization mode, if supported by appropriate data.

Use scientifically appropriate datasets where possible.

Do not pretend to render every galaxy in the observable universe individually.

The experience should communicate the universe's enormous scale and structure while clearly identifying when a visualization is a sample, simulation, illustration, or data-driven representation.

Allow users to rotate, pan, zoom, and select visible structures.

Selecting a structure should open a scientific information panel containing its name, available properties, source, and context.

## LAYER 2 — GALACTIC OVERVIEW

Transition into a view of galaxies and their surrounding environments.

Include available information such as:

- Galaxy names and identifiers.
- Galaxy classification.
- Celestial coordinates.
- Redshift and distance estimates, where available.
- Apparent magnitude, where available.
- Associated catalogues.
- Scientific imagery.
- Related objects.

Allow the user to select a galaxy and inspect its properties.

When possible, display real astronomical imagery alongside the spatial representation.

Distinguish between actual images, catalogue-derived positions, and generated artistic representations.

## LAYER 3 — MILKY WAY VIEW

Provide a navigable visualization of the Milky Way.

Where supported, show:

- The Galactic center.
- The Solar System's approximate position.
- Major galactic components.
- Selected star catalogues.
- Nebulae and star-forming regions.
- Globular clusters.
- Open clusters.
- Notable stellar systems.
- Available galactic coordinate information.

Use actual catalogue data when available.

Do not fabricate exact three-dimensional positions for objects whose distances are unknown or uncertain.

Where scientific knowledge is incomplete, communicate uncertainty rather than creating false precision.

## LAYER 4 — STELLAR NEIGHBORHOOD

Zoom into the Solar System's surrounding stellar neighborhood.

Include selected nearby stars and other relevant astronomical objects from available catalogues.

Support:

- Star selection.
- Distance inspection.
- Stellar classification.
- Catalogue identifiers.
- Available temperature, luminosity, and other stellar properties.
- Distance comparisons.
- Relative-position visualization.

If the interface displays a 3D arrangement, explain whether positions are measured, inferred, approximate, or illustrative.

## LAYER 5 — SOLAR SYSTEM

Transition into the Solar System.

Where supported, show:

- The Sun.
- Planets.
- Dwarf planets.
- Natural satellites.
- Asteroids.
- Comets.
- Selected small bodies.
- Orbital paths.
- Object labels.
- Relevant physical properties.

Provide controls for selecting individual objects and inspecting their available information.

Allow users to switch between an illustrative overview and more scientifically constrained orbital visualizations.

If orbital data are available, use appropriate ephemerides or orbital elements rather than arbitrary animation.

If orbital scales must be distorted for readability, provide an explicit scale indicator.

## LAYER 6 — PLANETARY AND MOON INSPECTION

Allow users to zoom toward a selected planet or moon.

Where data and performance allow, provide:

- Rotating 3D globes.
- Surface textures.
- Terrain or topography layers.
- Atmosphere effects.
- Natural satellite systems.
- Surface features.
- Day/night terminators.
- Available imagery.
- Physical properties.
- Scientific mission information.

Provide an inspector for selected planetary bodies.

Do not fabricate high-resolution terrain or claim that an artistic texture is actual surface imagery.

## LAYER 7 — EXOPLANETARY SYSTEMS

Let users investigate planetary systems beyond our Solar System.

Integrate appropriate catalogue information, such as confirmed exoplanets from NASA's Exoplanet Archive.

Where available, display:

- Host star.
- Confirmed planets.
- Planetary radius and mass.
- Orbital period.
- Semi-major axis.
- Discovery method.
- Discovery year.
- Distance from Earth.
- Available system parameters.

Where orbital parameters are known, create an educational system visualization.

Make it clear when planetary sizes, orbital distances, and orbital periods have been scaled for visibility.

Do not imply that an exoplanet has been directly imaged when the visualization is based only on catalogue parameters.

## LAYER 8 — DEEP-SPACE OBJECT INSPECTION

Provide a pathway to investigate selected deep-space objects.

Potential categories include:

- Nebulae.
- Galaxies.
- Star clusters.
- Supernova remnants.
- Pulsars.
- Black holes.
- Other well-documented astronomical objects.

Use available catalogues and scientifically attributed imagery.

When an object is selected, show relevant observations, scientific classifications, source links, and known uncertainties.

Do not imply that a black hole or distant object is being observed in real time merely because it appears in the interface.

